Agency is bounded attractor revision

A naïve system treats the goal as immutable.

A stronger system distinguishes:

* Goal
* Interpretation of the goal
* Current strategy
* Local action

Only some should remain fixed.

If the attractor itself can never be questioned, the agent becomes stubborn. If it changes too easily, the agent has no identity or continuity.

So Pantheon agency needs a hierarchy:

G_0 \supset G_1 \supset P_t \supset a_t

Where:

* G_0: constitutional invariants and hard boundaries.
* G_1: the active objective.
* P_t: the current plan.
* a_t: the next action.

Actions may change rapidly. Plans may change after feedback. Goals require stronger evidence. Constitutional invariants remain protected.

That is where clever initiative becomes reliable rather than merely energetic.

The compressed formulation

Agency is the capacity to preserve an objective through changing representations, limited resources, uncertain feedback, and repeated contact with reality.

Or in Pantheon language:

The attractor supplies will.
The pruner supplies memory.
The transducer supplies hands.
The validator supplies reality.
The invariant supplies identity.

That is no longer just a better prompt.

It is the outline of a bounded cognitive runtime.